  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday: Warm and dry conditions are expected\nthrough Friday as an upper level ridge builds. Temperatures \nwill be around the upper 80s to upper 90s through Friday. Today \nwill feature elevated to critical fire weather conditions as \nsurface high pressure in western Washington increases pressure \ngradients across the Cascades. Fire weather concerns will be \nmaximized across the lower Palouse and Snake River valley west \nof Lewiston and in the Cascade gaps once downvalley flow \ndominates in the late afternoon and early evening hours today. \nOverall expect breezy west winds around 10-15 mph with gusts \naround 20-25 mph for the Cascade gaps and the lower \nPalouse/Snake River valley. Thursday and Friday will have \nelevated fire weather conditions thanks to deep mixing around \n700-600 mb and temperatures reaching into the low to mid 90s \nacross the Cascades and northern mountain valleys. \n\nSaturday through Tuesday: Ensemble guidance continues to be in good\nagreement with troughing offshore Friday but the evolution of \nthis into the weekend is muddy at best. Latest EPS/CMC ensembles\n(70% of the global ensembles) keep the trough west of Oregon \nand more amplified Saturday, which would favor more chances for \nthunderstorms across the Inland Northwest. The latest GEFS \nensemble (30% of the global ensembles) still brings this trough\nand associated dry cold front through the Inland Northwest \nSaturday which favors a drier and windier scenario. Either way, \ncritical fire weather conditions appear likely at this stage as \nthunderstorms that form in the EPS/CMC environment would be \nmoving fairly quickly to the north with decently strong \nsoutherly 500 mb flow around 35-50 kt, increasing the chances \nfor dry lightning. In the GEFS solutions, critical fire weather \nconditions appear likely with sustained winds around 15-25 mph \nand relative humidities around 15-25%. Confidence will increase \nover the next couple of days how this time period will play out.\nTemperatures behind the front will be knocked down to near \nnormal in the 80s, but the exact evolution behind this trough \nremains unclear. /DB",
